A-16 large-screen portrait capture UI, on the zxing_api36 emulator forced to
1600x2560 at 240dpi (1066dp wide) -- the size the defect was first reproduced at.
2026-11-02

  adb shell wm size 1600x2560 && adb shell wm density 240
  ... and afterwards: adb shell wm size reset && adb shell wm density reset

CaptureActivity asks for sensorLandscape and Android 16 ignores it on a screen this
size, so the activity runs in a portrait window. That part cannot be fixed; what
follows is the UI being made to work in the window it is given.

== 1. The viewfinder rectangle ==

The two caps on the frame size, 1200 and 675, are five eighths of 1920 and of 1080:
the long and short sides of a landscape phone. They were written as a width cap and a
height cap, which is the same thing right up until the window is portrait.

  1600x2560 before:  1000 x 675  = 62% of the width, 26% of the height
  1600x2560 after:    675 x 1200 = 42% of the width, 47% of the height
  1920x1080 (phone):  1200 x 675 = 62% of both, before and after -- unchanged

The caps now follow the window's long and short axes rather than x and y. On any
landscape window the long axis IS x, so nothing about the case everybody has moves;
FramingRectSizerTest pins that as well as the portrait case.

  reports/a16-portrait-scan-before.png   a wide shallow band
  reports/a16-portrait-scan-after.png    a frame the shape of its window

== 2. The status line ==

It is anchored to the bottom of the window. On a landscape phone that is just under
the frame; on a 2560px-tall window it is a thousand pixels below it. In a portrait
window it now sits directly under the frame.

  status_view bounds, 1600x2560, after:  [533,1892][1067,1969]
  the frame's bottom edge:               1880
  status_view bounds on DN-ENG41 (960x640, landscape): [79,544][881,592]
    -- the same as before this change; the bottom of a 640px window.

This needed a third attempt to be right and the reason is worth keeping. The frame is
a camera property: it does not exist when the layout is inflated, nor when onResume
runs, nor at the layout pass that follows -- the surface has not been created and the
driver has not been opened yet. The first two versions read a null rectangle and did
nothing at all, silently. It now retries from a pre-draw listener and removes that
listener the moment it succeeds.

== 3. The result screen ==

The result row is the thumbnail and its metadata beside the scanned text, which is
right in a wide window and wrong in a tall one. It stacks when the window is
portrait. Done in code rather than in a layout-port variant: a second copy of
capture.xml is exactly how A-18 happened, and layout-ldpi/capture.xml has the two new
ids added for the same reason.

  reports/a16-portrait-result-after.png

The first version of this stacked the row and lost the scanned text off the bottom of
the screen: the metadata column is fill_parent tall, which centres it beside the text
in a row and eats the entire height in a column. Format, type and time were there and
the URL -- the only thing the user scanned for -- was not. The column is wrap_content
in portrait now.

